Part 2

KEY DECISIONS ALREADY MADE (Don’t Re-Litigate These):
These were each discussed and settled already — if a new session suggests reconsidering any
of these, that’s a signal something’s off, not a reason to actually redo the discussion:
Why This Project Exists At All;
Two prior directions were considered and moved away from:
● Universal Index (ecosystem discovery platform) — shelved because lukso.tools already
serves this purpose and has official in-wallet adoption
● Tessera (soulbound reputation protocol) — paused, not abandoned, because launching
a project touching real staked funds as a first public release, with no prior on-chain track
record, carries real reputational risk
UP Health is the deliberate low-risk, high-utility project to build first, establishing credibility
before returning to Tessera.
Core Design Principles (Non-Negotiable):
1. Diagnose, don’t treat — never edit permissions directly, always link out to the existing
LSP6 permission manager for fixes
2. Identity-matched gating, not simple wallet-gating — sensitive flags only shown when the
connected wallet matches the queried profile, not merely “any wallet connected.”
Anonymous/mismatched lookups only see basic, non-sensitive info.
3. Stay lean — no database, no persistent backend, everything read live from-chain per
lookup
Confirmed Scope Decisions:
● Network: LUKSO Mainnet only. No testnet, no other chains, unless LUKSO itself
launches a publicly-discussed cross-ecosystem integration in the future.
● Vaults: LSP9 Vaults are supported quietly in v1 (same underlying LSP6 mechanism as
Profiles, so it’s nearly free) — but with no dedicated UI/marketing calling attention to it as
a distinct feature.
● Multisig detection: Deferred to Phase 2 (post-launch). v1 only does basic “is this a
contract or a wallet” detection, not specific multisig-pattern recognition — even though
research confirmed multisig-as-controller is real and already used in LUKSO’s own
official recovery infrastructure.
● Wallet connection: Two genuinely different connection paths — @lukso/up-provider for
Grid-embedded context, standard browser-extension connection for standalone visits —
converging on the same identity-matching logic once connected. This is explicitly not one
unified mechanism; it’s two paths built to feel like one experience.
● Naming: “UP Health,” not “UP Doctor” — because “Doctor” implies treatment, and this
tool only diagnoses.
Research Findings Already Resolved (Don’t Re-Research These):

● LUKSO’s own recovery-controller pattern confirms that “a controller can add other
controllers” is not inherently risky on its own (it mirrors LUKSO’s own official design) —
it’s only flagged when bundled with broad execution/value-transfer permissions at the
same time
● A profile with only one controller and no recovery path is a legitimate flag, since
LUKSO’s own architecture explicitly recommends against that
● Multisig-as-controller is confirmed real and already used in LUKSO’s own infrastructure
(Safe-based Recovery Profile), which is why it’s slated for Phase 2 rather than dismissed
entirely — the deferral is about detection complexity, not about it being rare
● LUKSO’s Mainnet block explorer runs on Blockscout, whose API conventions are
consistent across other networks’ deployments — but the exact endpoint path still needs
confirming against LUKSO’s specific instance once you reach that step (Handbook
Phase 3, Step 3.5)

